      "summary": "This study describes a three-step continuous flow sequence involving diazonium salt formation and azide chemistry performed in a copper-coil reactor with PFA tubing (1.0 mm ID, 20 mL volume). The process operates at 120°C with a 30-minute residence time, 0.67 mL/min flow rate, and 0.45 M concentration in dichloromethane (DCM) solvent, using an 8 bar back-pressure regulator. The flow process achieved 95% yield compared to 80% yield in the batch baseline at 80°C, representing a 15% improvement in yield while enabling higher reaction temperatures. The copper-coil reactor design with precise temperature and residence time control allowed for better heat management and safer handling of potentially hazardous diazonium intermediates. Flow chemistry enabled the integration of multiple synthetic steps in a continuous sequence, improving overall process efficiency and scalability compared to traditional batch operations.",
